Z||000000||Notchit||12-30-2000||03:34 AM||||Hmmm....well now that i've been starting my car more often (can't wait till spring  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<BR>i've noticed a nice little "puddle" of anti-freeze and such under the nose of my car. it starts to leak after the engine has ran for a bit (10-15 mins) and i tried to find the cause, i was thinking hopefully it was a hose, but it seems to be coming from the bottom of the rad. should I get it re-cored? or pressure tested? I also heard that putting a teaspoon or so of pepper will stop a leak....thats a last ditch effort  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>also, has anyone else had this problem?<BR>thanks<BR><P>------------------<BR>Seein Red<BR>'85 Fiero GT<BR>ICQ#: 56708070<BR>_________________________||||reg||11
Z||000001||terryk||12-30-2000||03:53 AM||||If it's a side tank, you're hosed. Verify that it's really the source (and not a hose leaking down to the bottom of the mounts).<P>If it is, get another one. New ones aren't that bad.<P>It's worth pulling out and having it checked. It might still be repairable.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Notchit||12-30-2000||04:04 AM||||yeah, I think i might as well get it checked. I can see that it is leaking from the bottom of the rad, on the drivers side (i can't SEE the leak, but thats where its comming from) if i get it checked, its just more money lost, but for a great cause i guess, just to see the look on those Neon drivers faces when I race past 'em <BR>Its below zero here (degrees) so below freezing point, if I pulled out the rad, that could lead to a cracked block correct? so I may have to wait a bit.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Verticalspeed||12-30-2000||04:37 AM|||| What are the prices for new radiators? Has anyone tried a radiator from a different car?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||12-30-2000||09:38 AM||||If you pull the radiator now, it won't lead to a cracked block as there will be the same fluid in it. Cracked blocks are caused by incorrect or no anitfreeze mixtures.<BR>Pull it now if you wish, stay warm!||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||12-30-2000||10:41 AM||||Another thing that can cause this is cheap radiator hoses. It's possible that the metal radiator contracts more than the hoses in extreme cold and causes a gap between the hose and the radiator inlet leading to a leak.<P>I had this problem on another car...in extreme cold there was always a puddle under the radiator and even retightening the clamps didn't solve the problem. I switched to the higher priced Gates hoses and the leak stopped.<P>Hoses that are more than a couple of years old can do this as well, especially given the temp extremes in your area.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-30-2000||12:21 PM||||goto carpart.com or wrenchead and check prices there. then call a local store. Last I looked a new one was between $100-200.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Notchit||12-30-2000||04:32 PM||||Yeah, its pretty cold here, the past few weeks its been dipping down to -30 (and the car still starts awesome!) I never really saw any coolent when it was warm...so i'm hoping that its a hose. the cars 15 years old, all origional, i mean its time something broke i guess, but I can't find any lines of coolant that indicate its coming from another source (hose) its coming from the bottom of the rad, where it sits, and leaking off a corner, and its not much just a little drip every three or so seconds..<BR><P>------------------<BR>Seein Red<BR>'85 Fiero GT<BR>ICQ#: 56708070<BR>_________________________||||reg||4
Z||000008||William Federle||12-30-2000||05:17 PM||||I had to replace my radiator, water pump, and hoses last summer. I got everything changed out and stopped all the leaks from loose clamps, and I still noticed a little green liquid after I had been driving enough for the car to warm up - the liquid was on the bottom of the new radiator. It turned out to be from the connection of the hose from the overflow tank to the radiator neck. I hadn't put a clamp on it since it seemed to fit snugly. After I clamped it with tie-wraps because there wasn't enough room for a steel clamp, the leak stopped. I think that no matter where on the radiator the leak is coming from it will find its way to the piece that the radiator sits in. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Notchit||12-30-2000||05:20 PM||||True, would I be able to lift the rad, while its running and try to see where its coming from?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Notchit||12-30-2000||05:20 PM||||True, would I be able to lift the rad, while its running and try to see where its coming from?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phaeton||12-31-2000||04:08 PM||||GM sells coolant dye that glows in ultraviolet light.  The dye is cheap.  Now take the blacklight you have hanging over your posters and go out to the Fiero while it is pitch black out.  The fluid at the bottom should have a glowing tail leading to the original source.||||reg||12
